 "timeline": {
  "current_model": "AATF/Che-1 is a multifunctional nuclear phosphoprotein that binds RNA polymerase II (and I), Rb, and multiple transcription factors; in unstressed cells it promotes proliferation by displacing HDAC1 from Rb/E2F and Sp1 complexes to activate E2F target genes and p21, while upon genotoxic stress it is phosphorylated by ATM/ATR, Chk2, MK2, CK2, and HIPK2 at distinct residues\u2014triggering nuclear translocation, promoter switching from E2F targets to p53/p21 loci to activate cell-cycle arrest, or (when phosphorylated by MK2) binding PUMA/BAX/BAK promoters to repress p53-driven apoptosis\u2014and is ultimately degraded via Pin1/HDM2/HIPK2-mediated ubiquitylation during apoptosis; AATF also forms the ANN complex (with NGDN and NOL10) in nucleoli to support 40S ribosomal subunit biogenesis and rRNA processing, localizes to paraspeckles via NEAT1_2 to resolve R-loops and suppress interferon activation, stabilizes XRCC4 for NHEJ repair (released by ATM-mediated Ser189 phosphorylation at DNA breaks), and interacts with partners including Par-4, Tau, Cdk5, c-Jun, AIF, SAGA subunits, and histones to regulate apoptosis, neuronal function, and chromatin acetylation across multiple cellular contexts."
